      <header class="article-header">
        <h1>Evaluating Data Centers and Residential Development</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          A Comparative Framework for Fiscal, Infrastructure, and Utility Impacts
        </p>
      </header>


      <section class="article-body">

        <!-- Executive Summary -->
        <h2>Executive Summary</h2>

        <p>
          Communities evaluating new development often face a difficult question:
          which types of growth create the greatest long-term public value?
          Residential development and large data centers create value in very
          different ways. Residential development spreads economic activity across
          households, local businesses, and public services, while data centers
          concentrate substantial capital investment and infrastructure demand at
          a single site.
        </p>

        <p>
          Using Jefferson County, Alabama, as an illustrative residential benchmark,
          a 1,000-home development represents approximately $240 million in residential
          property value and about $1.4 million in estimated annual property taxes.
          Based on median household income of $69,346, those households also represent
          approximately $69.3 million in aggregate annual income.
        </p>

        <p>
          If 40% to 60% of that income were spent on purchases subject to Alabama's
          4% general state sales-tax rate, the illustrative state sales-tax contribution
          would be approximately $1.1 million to $1.7 million annually. Combined with
          the property-tax estimate, the two identified tax categories create a
          residential comparison benchmark of approximately $2.5 million to
          $3.1 million annually before local taxes, other revenue, and public-service costs.
        </p>

        <aside class="note" aria-label="Methodology note">
          <strong>Important:</strong>
          The sales-tax estimate is an illustrative scenario rather than a forecast
          of actual collections. The analysis assumes that 40% to 60% of aggregate
          household income is spent on purchases subject to Alabama's 4% general
          state sales-tax rate.
        </aside>

        <p>
          The selected Alabama data-center projects used in this comparison represent
          announced investments ranging from approximately $372 million to $14.5 billion,
          with a median of $1.5 billion and a simple average of approximately
          $3.75 billion.
        </p>

        <p>
          The selected projects therefore operate at a substantially larger capital
          scale than the illustrative residential development. Capital investment
          alone, however, does not determine which form of development creates greater
          public value. The comparison depends on how investment translates into
          recurring tax revenue, utility economics, employment, infrastructure
          requirements, incentives, and public-service costs.
        </p>


        <!-- Comparison Table -->
        <h2>Comparison of Identified Benefits and Performance Conditions</h2>

        <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Development comparison table" tabindex="0">
