        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <h2>1. What Each Development Brings to a Community</h2>

        <p>
          Residential development distributes economic activity across households
          and businesses. Residents pay property taxes and utility bills, make taxable
          purchases, register vehicles, and support nearby employers. Home construction
          can also generate permits, inspection fees, connection charges, contractor
          activity, and taxable purchases.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those benefits must also be considered alongside the continuing costs of
          serving residential growth, including schools, roads, public safety,
          sanitation, stormwater management, water, and wastewater service.
        </p>

        <p>
          A data center creates a different economic profile. Instead of distributing
          investment among hundreds or thousands of households, a data center
          concentrates significant capital investment and utility demand at a single
          site. Potential benefits can include property taxes retained after incentives,
          construction activity, permanent employment, supplier activity, and
          infrastructure investment.
        </p>

        <p>
          Data-center development can also create substantial infrastructure
          requirements. A balanced analysis therefore needs to consider incentives,
          public infrastructure, utility requirements, water and sewer needs, and
          the continuing costs associated with supporting the facility.
        </p>

        <p>
          The expanded Meta campus in Montgomery illustrates the distinction between
          capital scale and employment impact. Public announcements associated with the
          project reported more than $1.5 billion in investment, more than 100 operational
          jobs, and more than 1,500 skilled-trade workers at peak construction.
          Capital investment, temporary construction activity, permanent employment,
          and recurring tax collections should therefore be evaluated as separate
          measures rather than treated as interchangeable benefits.
        </p>


        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <h2>2. When Data-Center Investment Produces Public Value</h2>

        <p>
          Data-center investment creates public value when large capital commitments
          translate into measurable long-term benefits such as retained tax revenue,
          employment, supplier activity, infrastructure investment, and sustainable
          utility economics.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alabama's Chapter 9B program allows qualifying projects to receive abatements
          of specified state and local sales, use, and non-educational property taxes.
          For qualifying data-processing centers receiving abatements before
          January 1, 2027, the published framework provides potential 10-, 20-, or
          30-year abatement periods based on applicable investment thresholds.
        </p>

        <p>
          Incentives make it especially important to distinguish between the size of
          an announced investment and the amount of recurring public revenue that
          ultimately remains with state and local governments. State, county,
          municipal, school, and other public benefits may differ depending on the
          structure of the project and applicable incentive agreements.
        </p>

        <p>
          Separate Alabama Jobs Act incentives may also affect the comparison.
          Published program materials describe a Jobs Credit based on eligible
          Alabama-resident payroll and an Investment Credit of up to 1.5% annually
          of qualified capital investment, subject to eligibility, duration, and
          approval requirements.
        </p>

        <p>
          Utility economics represent another part of the analysis. From a
          utility-economics perspective, large-load development can be evaluated by
          comparing the revenue associated with new electricity demand against the
          incremental generation, transmission, distribution, and other infrastructure
          required to serve it.
        </p>

        <p>
          A more complete project-level comparison would require additional inputs,
          including assessed value, taxes retained after incentives, permanent payroll,
          infrastructure requirements, public-service costs, and the incremental costs
          and revenues associated with utility service.
        </p>


        <!-- Section 3 -->
        <h2>3. Conditions Under Which Each Development Performs Better</h2>

        <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Development performance conditions table" tabindex="0">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Conditions that influence the relative performance of residential and data-center development
            </caption>

            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Performance Condition</th>
                <th scope="col">Residential Development Performs Better When</th>
                <th scope="col">Data Center Performs Better When</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Recurring tax revenue</th>
                <td>
                  Net recurring taxes and related revenue exceed the public-service
                  costs associated with residential growth.
                </td>
                <td>
                  Retained annual taxes after incentives exceed the comparable
                  residential contribution after relevant project costs are considered.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Utility economics</th>
                <td>
                  Residential utility revenue produces a positive contribution after
                  the incremental costs of providing electric, water, and sewer service.
                </td>
                <td>
                  Revenue associated with the large load exceeds the incremental costs
                  of infrastructure and service required to support it.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Infrastructure</th>
                <td>
                  Developer contributions and recurring public revenue cover the
                  additional infrastructure and services required by residential growth.
                </td>
                <td>
                  Project-related infrastructure requirements are appropriately funded
                  and do not outweigh the project's recurring public benefits.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Water use</th>
                <td>
                  Residential development creates greater net public value relative
                  to its water and wastewater requirements.
                </td>
                <td>
                  The project's economic contribution remains favorable after its
                  water, wastewater, and infrastructure requirements are considered.
                </td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Overall result</th>
                <td>
                  Residential development creates the greater recurring net public
                  and economic benefit.
                </td>
                <td>
                  The data center creates the greater recurring net public and
                  economic benefit.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>


        <!-- Utility and Infrastructure Considerations -->
        <h2>4. Utility and Infrastructure Considerations</h2>

        <p>
          Utility performance should be evaluated on the basis of net contribution
          rather than gross revenue alone. A complete analysis would consider expected
          electricity demand, required infrastructure, generation and transmission
          needs, cost recovery, and the incremental costs associated with serving
          the new load.
        </p>

        <p>
          Water and wastewater systems require similar analysis. Revenue generated by
          new customers should be considered alongside system capacity, treatment,
          pumping, infrastructure, and operating costs.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, Jefferson County sewer bills include both fixed and volumetric
          charges based partly on metered water use. The county generally applies a
          return factor for residential customers to recognize that some water use
          does not return to the sewer system.
        </p>

        <p>
          Community performance should therefore be measured using a broader set of
          outcomes than any single metric. Tax revenue, economic activity, infrastructure
          costs, utility requirements, public services, employment, and resource use
          all contribute to the final comparison.
        </p>


