                <td>Approximately $1.4 million</td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Illustrative Alabama state sales tax</th>
                <td>Approximately $1.1 million</td>
                <td>Approximately $1.7 million</td>
              </tr>

              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Identified annual total</th>
                <td>Approximately $2.5 million</td>
                <td>Approximately $3.1 million</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The identified residential tax categories therefore total approximately
          $2.5 million to $3.1 million annually. This figure establishes an
          illustrative comparison benchmark rather than a complete break-even point
          or forecast of actual collections.
        </p>

        <p>
          The benchmark excludes local taxes, other forms of public revenue,
          construction activity, broader economic effects, utility contribution,
          and public-service costs. A complete development comparison should add
          those categories consistently for both types of development.
        </p>


        <!-- Conclusion -->
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
          Using Jefferson County benchmarks, an illustrative 1,000-home development
          represents approximately $240 million in residential property value and
          approximately $2.5 million to $3.1 million in identified annual property
          and Alabama state sales taxes before other revenues and public-service costs.
        </p>

        <p>
          The selected Alabama data-center projects operate at a much larger capital
          scale, with a median announced investment of approximately $1.5 billion
          and a simple average of approximately $3.75 billion. That difference is
          meaningful, but capital scale alone does not determine the better
          development outcome.
        </p>

        <p>
          A data center creates stronger results when its retained recurring taxes,
          employment and supplier activity, infrastructure contributions, and other
          measurable economic benefits outweigh the costs associated with supporting
          the project. Residential development creates stronger results when recurring
          tax revenue, household economic activity, utility contribution, and related
          growth produce the greater benefit after the continuing costs of public
          services and infrastructure are considered.
        </p>

        <p>
          In other words, the more useful policy question is not simply whether a
          community should prefer homes or data centers. It is whether a particular
          development converts its economic advantages into a sustainable net benefit
          for the community supporting it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Capital scale favors the selected data-center projects. Residential
          development distributes economic activity more broadly across households,
          businesses, and public services. Determining which performs better requires
          comparing those different advantages on a consistent basis.
        </p>


